son-huang.html?partner=rss&amp;emc=rss</link><guid isPermaLink="true">https://www.nytimes.com/2026/11/16/technology/nvidia-jensen-madison-huang.html?partner=rss&amp;emc=rss</guid><atom:link rel="standout" href="https://www.nytimes.com/2026/11/16/technology/nvidia-jensen-madison-huang.html?partner=rss&amp;emc=rss" /><description>Installed as her father’s de facto chief of staff, Jensen Huang’s daughter alienated top executives. Now her lingering role poses a test for interim C.E.O. Colette Kress.</description><dc:creator>Kalley Huang, Tripp Mickle and Ryan Mac</dc:creator><pubDate>Mon, 16 Nov 2026 23:29:54 GMT</pubDate><category domain="http://www.nytimes.com/namespaces/keywords/nyt_des">NVIDIA</category><category domain="http://www.nytimes.com/namespaces/keywords/nyt_des">Workplace</category><category domain="http://www.nytimes.com/namespaces/keywords/nyt_des">Megaspeed</category><category domain="http://www.nytimes.com/namespaces/keywords/nyt_des">Alice Huang</category><category domain="http://www.nytimes.com/namespaces/keywords/nyt_des">Colette Kress</category><category domain="http://www.nytimes.com/namespaces/keywords/nyt_des">Jen-Hsun Huang</category><category domain="http://www.nytimes.com/namespaces/keywords/nyt_des">Jensen Huang</category><category domain="http://www.nytimes.com/namespaces/keywords/nyt_des">Lori Huang</category><category domain="http://www.nytimes.com/namespaces/keywords/nyt_des">Ian Buck</category><category domain="http://www.nytimes.com/namespaces/keywords/nyt_des">London Business School</category><category domain="http://www.nytimes.com/namespaces/keywords/nyt_des">Adultery</category><category domain="http://www.nytimes.com/namespaces/keywords/nyt_des">Appointments and Executive Changes</category><category domain="http://www.nytimes.com/namespaces/keywords/nyt_des">Corporate espionage</category><category domain="http://www.nytimes.com/namespaces/keywords/nyt_des">China</category><category domain="http://www.nytimes.com/namespaces/keywords/nyt_des">US</category><media:content url="https://static01.nyt.com/images/2026/11/16/multimedia/BIZ-MADISON-HUANG-1-01-cmjw/BIZ-MADISON-HUANG-1-01-cmjw-videoSixteenByNineJumbo1600-v2.jpg" medium="image"><media:credit>Maxim Shemetov/Reuters</media:credit><media:description>Madison Huang, a senior director of product marketing at Nvidia and daughter of Jensen Huang, at the 2026 World Robot Conference, in Beijing in August.</media:description></media:content></item><item><title>Jensen Huang’s Resignation From Nvidia Worries the A.I. Start-Up World</title><link>https://www.nytimes.com/2026/11/13/technology/jensen-huang-silicon-valley-start-ups.html?partner=rss&amp;emc=rss</link><guid isPermaLink="true">https://www.nytimes.com/2026/11/13/technology/jensen-huang-silicon-valley-start-ups.html?partner=rss&amp;emc=rss</guid><atom:link rel="standout" href="https://www.nytimes.com/2026/11/13/technology/jensen-huang-silicon-valley-start-ups.html?partner=rss&amp;emc=rss" /><description>Mr. Huang championed start-ups, with Nvidia holding $48 billion worth of stakes in 229 private companies, including OpenAI and Anthropic, at the end of July.</description><dc:creator>Erin Griffith and Cory Weinberg</dc:creator><pubDate>Fri, 13 Nov 2026 16:06:55 GMT</pubDate><category domain="http://www.nytimes.com/namespaces/keywords/nyt_des">Jen-Hsun Huang</category><category domain="http://www.nytimes.com/namespaces/keywords/nyt_des">Jensen Huang</category><category domain="http://www.nytimes.com/namespaces/keywords/nyt_des">NVIDIA</category><category domain="http://www.nytimes.com/namespaces/keywords/nyt_des">Startup</category><category domain="http://www.nytimes.com/namespaces/keywords/nyt_des">Computers and the Internet</category><category domain="http://www.nytimes.com/namespaces/keywords/nyt_des">Tech Industry</category><category domain="http://www.nytimes.com/namespaces/keywords/nyt_des">Artificial intelligence</category><category domain="http://www.nytimes.com/namespaces/keywords/nyt_des">Venture capital</category><category domain="http://www.nytimes.com/namespaces/keywords/nyt_des">Cloud computing</category><category domain="http://www.nytimes.com/namespaces/keywords/nyt_des">Entrepreneurship</category><category domain="http://www.nytimes.com/namespaces/keywords/nyt_des">Computer Chip</category><category domain="http://www.nytimes.com/namespaces/keywords/nyt_des">Anthropic</category><category domain="http://www.nytimes.com/namespaces/keywords/nyt_des">OpenAI</category><media:content url="https://static01.nyt.com/images/2026/11/13/multimedia/13biz-nvidia-startups-2-mfvc/13biz-nvidia-startups-2-mfvc-videoSixteenByNineJumbo1600.jpg" medium="image"><media:credit>Mike Kai Chen for The New York Times</media:credit><media:description>Jensen Huang’s resignation on Thursday as chief executive of Nvidia has investors and start-up founders nervous that they have lost a powerful ally.</media:description></media:content></item><item><title>Tech Leaders Rally Behind Jensen Huang</title><link>https://www.nytimes.com/2026/11/14/technology/silicon-valley-nvidia-jensen-huang.html?partner=rss&amp;emc=rss</link><guid isPermaLink="true">https://www.nytimes.com/2026/11/14/technology/silicon-valley-nvidia-jensen-huang.html?partner=rss&amp;emc=rss</guid><atom:link rel="standout" href="https://www.nytimes.com/2026/11/14/technology/silicon-valley-nvidia-jensen-huang.html?partner=rss&amp;emc=rss" /><description>In social media posts and letters to Nvidia’s board, Mr. Huang’s billionaire peers described him as a singular leader who could not be replaced at Nvidia.</description><dc:creator>Kalley Huang</dc:creator><pubDate>Sat, 14 Nov 2026 18:29:56 GMT</pubDate><category domain="http://www.nytimes.com/namespaces/keywords/nyt_des">NVIDIA</category><category domain="http://www.nytimes.com/namespaces/keywords/nyt_des">Jen-Hsun Huang</category><category domain="http://www.nytimes.com/namespaces/keywords/nyt_des">Jensen Huang</category><category domain="http://www.nytimes.com/namespaces/keywords/nyt_des">Artificial intelligence</category><category domain="http://www.nytimes.com/namespaces/keywords/nyt_des">Computer Chip</category><category domain="http://www.nytimes.com/namespaces/keywords/nyt_des">Board of directors</category><category domain="http://www.nytimes.com/namespaces/keywords/nyt_des">Appointments and Executive Changes</category><category domain="http://www.nytimes.com/namespaces/keywords/nyt_des">Computers and the Internet</category><category domain="http://www.nytimes.com/namespaces/keywords/nyt_des">Tech Industry</category><category domain="http://www.nytimes.com/namespaces/keywords/nyt_des">Ethics Misconduct Malfeasance</category><category domain="http://www.nytimes.com/namespaces/keywords/nyt_des">Adultery</category><category domain="http://www.nytimes.com/namespaces/keywords/nyt_des">China</category><category domain="http://www.nytimes.com/namespaces/keywords/nyt_des">Corporate espionage</category><category domain="http://www.nytimes.com/namespaces/keywords/nyt_des">Spying and Intelligence Agencies</category><media:content url="https://static01.nyt.com/images/2026/11/14/multimedia/14biz-jensen-support-Huang-vpjh/14biz-jensen-support-Huang-vpjh-videoSixteenByNineJumbo1600.jpg" medium="image"><media:credit>John Macdougall/Agence France-Presse — Getty Images</media:credit><media:description>“Jensen drove supply chain brilliantly at Nvidia, but many of those deals were a handshake with Jensen, not a written contract,” Elon Musk said of Jensen Huang in a social media post.</media:description></media:content></item><item><title>Put Your A.I. Agents to Work with These 5 Killer Tasks</title><link>https://www.nytimes.com/2026/10/25/technology/personaltech/openai-dots-meta-muse-killer-tasks.html?partner=rss&amp;emc=rss</link><guid isPermaLink="true">https://www.nytimes.com/2026/10/25/technology/personaltech/openai-dots-meta-muse-killer-tasks.html?partner=rss&amp;emc=rss</guid><atom:link rel="standout" href="https://www.nytimes.com/2026/10/25/technology/personaltech/openai-dots-meta-muse-killer-tasks.html?partner=rss&amp;emc=rss" /><description>When you start using Meta’s Muse and OpenAI’s dots for these tasks — like applying for jobs and booking hard-to-get restaurant tables — you may never go back.</description><dc:creator>Brian X. Chen</dc:creator><pubDate>Sun, 25 Oct 2026 09:01:00 GMT</pubDate><category domain="http://www.nytimes.com/namespaces/keywords/nyt_des">Artificial intelligence</category><category domain="http://www.nytimes.com/namespaces/keywords/nyt_des">Mobile Apps</category><category domain="http://www.nytimes.com/namespaces/keywords/nyt_des">Service Content</category><category domain="http://www.nytimes.com/namespaces/keywords/nyt_des">Meta</category><category domain="http://www.nytimes.com/namespaces/keywords/nyt_des">OpenAI</category><category domain="http://www.nytimes.com/namespaces/keywords/nyt_des">Cooking</category><category domain="http://www.nytimes.com/namespaces/keywords/nyt_des">Reservations</category><category domain="http://www.nytimes.com/namespaces/keywords/nyt_des">Restaurant</category><category domain="http://www.nytimes.com/namespaces/keywords/nyt_des">Exercise</category><category domain="http://www.nytimes.com/namespaces/keywords/nyt_des">Fitness</category><category domain="http://www.nytimes.com/namespaces/keywords/nyt_des">Job Recruiting and Hiring</category><media:content url="https://static01.nyt.com/images/2026/10/16/business/00techfix/00techfix-videoSixteenByNineJumbo1600.jpg" medium="image"><media:credit>Sisi Yu</media:credit></media:content></item><item><title>How Jensen Huang’s Secret Led to His Downfall at Nvidia</title><link>https://www.nytimes.com/2026/11/14/technology/jensen-huang-nvidia-board.html?partner=rss&amp;emc=rss</link><guid isPermaLink="true">https://www.nytimes.com/2026/11/14/technology/jensen-huang-nvidia-board.html?partner=rss&amp;emc=rss</guid><atom:link rel="standout" href="https://www.nytimes.com/2026/11/14/technology/jensen-huang-nvidia-board.html?partner=rss&amp;emc=rss" /><description>Nvidia’s leader said his relationship with a female customer was just about business. But what he was concealing eventually led to his resignation from the company he created.</description><dc:creator>Tripp Mickle, Ana Swanson, Paul Mozur, Jonathan Swan, Julian E. Barnes and Maggie Haberman</dc:creator><pubDate>Sat, 14 Nov 2026 21:50:17 GMT</pubDate><category domain="http://www.nytimes.com/namespaces/keywords/nyt_des">Artificial intelligence</category><category domain="http://www.nytimes.com/namespaces/keywords/nyt_des">Computer Chip</category><category domain="http://www.nytimes.com/namespaces/keywords/nyt_des">US Foreign Policy</category><category domain="http://www.nytimes.com/namespaces/keywords/nyt_des">Corporate espionage</category><category domain="http://www.nytimes.com/namespaces/keywords/nyt_des">Adultery</category><category domain="http://www.nytimes.com/namespaces/keywords/nyt_des">Spying and Intelligence Agencies</category><category domain="http://www.nytimes.com/namespaces/keywords/nyt_des">US Politics</category><category domain="http://www.nytimes.com/namespaces/keywords/nyt_des">Appointments and Executive Changes</category><category domain="http://www.nytimes.com/namespaces/keywords/nyt_des">Board of directors</category><category domain="http://www.nytimes.com/namespaces/keywords/nyt_des">Jen-Hsun Huang</category><category domain="http://www.nytimes.com/namespaces/keywords/nyt_des">Jensen Huang</category><category domain="http://www.nytimes.com/namespaces/keywords/nyt_des">Alice Huang</category><category domain="http://www.nytimes.com/namespaces/keywords/nyt_des">Donald Trump</category><category domain="http://www.nytimes.com/namespaces/keywords/nyt_des">Marco Rubio</category><category domain="http://www.nytimes.com/namespaces/keywords/nyt_des">Megaspeed</category><category domain="http://www.nytimes.com/namespaces/keywords/nyt_geo">Ministry of State Security of the People's Republic of China</category><category domain="http://www.nytimes.com/namespaces/keywords/nyt_des">NVIDIA</category><category domain="http://www.nytimes.com/namespaces/keywords/nyt_des">China</category><category domain="http://www.nytimes.com/namespaces/keywords/nyt_des">Singapore</category><media:content url="https://static01.nyt.com/images/2026/11/13/multimedia/BIZ-JENSEN-CRASH-1-01-ljmc/BIZ-JENSEN-CRASH-1-01-ljmc-videoSixteenByNineJumbo1600-v2.jpg" medium="image"><media:credit>Mike Kai Chen for The New York Times</media:credit><media:description>Jensen Huang stepped down as chief executive of Nvidia on Thursday.</media:description></media:content></item><item><title>Apple Was Late to the Video Podcast Boom. Now It’s Trying to Catch Up.</title><link>https://www.nytimes.com/2026/11/16/business/media/apple-tv-podcasts-youtube.html?partner=rss&amp;emc=rss</link><guid isPermaLink="true">https://www.nytimes.com/2026/11/16/business/media/apple-tv-podcasts-youtube.html?partner=rss&amp;emc=rss</guid><atom:link rel="standout" href="https://www.nytimes.com/2026/11/16/business/media/apple-tv-podcasts-youtube.html?partner=rss&amp;emc=rss" /><description>They will soon be free to all Apple TV users and will appear on more than one billion devices that have the app, including Rokus and smart TVs.</description><dc:creator>Sapna Maheshwari and Kalley Huang</dc:creator><pubDate>Mon, 16 Nov 2026 10:01:52 GMT</pubDate><category domain="http://www.nytimes.com/namespaces/keywords/nyt_des">Video Recordings</category><category domain="http://www.nytimes.com/namespaces/keywords/nyt_des">Downloads and Streaming</category><category domain="http://www.nytimes.com/namespaces/keywords/nyt_des">Social Media</category><category domain="http://www.nytimes.com/namespaces/keywords/nyt_des">Podcast</category><category domain="http://www.nytimes.com/namespaces/keywords/nyt_des">Apple</category><category domain="http://www.nytimes.com/namespaces/keywords/nyt_des">Edison Research</category><category domain="http://www.nytimes.com/namespaces/keywords/nyt_des">Apple TV</category><category domain="http://www.nytimes.com/namespaces/keywords/nyt_des">Sam Fragoso</category><category domain="http://www.nytimes.com/namespaces/keywords/nyt_des">Pete Davidson</category><category domain="http://www.nytimes.com/namespaces/keywords/nyt_des">Ina Garten</category><category domain="http://www.nytimes.com/namespaces/keywords/nyt_des">Amy Poehler</category><media:content url="https://static01.nyt.com/images/2026/11/16/business/16BIZ-APPLE-PODCASTS-2/16BIZ-APPLE-PODCASTS-2-videoSixteenByNineJumbo1600.png" medium="image"><media:credit>Apple</media:credit><media:description>Apple will bring video podcasts to Apple TV and increase the number of video podcasts on its regular Podcasts app. </media:description></media:content></item><item><title>A More Precise Clock Is on Its Way</title><link>https://www.nytimes.com/2026/11/17/fashion/optical-atomic-clocks-rolex.html?partner=rss&amp;emc=rss</link><guid isPermaLink="true">https://www.nytimes.com/2026/11/17/fashion/optical-atomic-clocks-rolex.html?partner=rss&amp;emc=rss</guid><atom:link rel="standout" href="https://www.nytimes.com/2026/11/17/fashion/optical-atomic-clocks-rolex.html?partner=rss&amp;emc=rss" /><description>The optical atomic clock, which scientists say would be off by about a second over 14 billion years, is soon expected to become a primary reference.</description><dc:creator>Penelope Colston</dc:creator><pubDate>Tue, 17 Nov 2026 10:00:32 GMT</pubDate><category domain="http://www.nytimes.com/namespaces/keywords/nyt_des">Watches and Clocks</category><category domain="http://www.nytimes.com/namespaces/keywords/nyt_des">Time</category><category domain="http://www.nytimes.com/namespaces/keywords/nyt_des">GPS</category><category domain="http://www.nytimes.com/namespaces/keywords/nyt_des">International Bureau of Weights and Measures</category><category domain="http://www.nytimes.com/namespaces/keywords/nyt_des">National Institute of Standards and Technology</category><category domain="http://www.nytimes.com/namespaces/keywords/nyt_des">Rolex</category><category domain="http://www.nytimes.com/namespaces/keywords/nyt_des">your-feed-fashion</category><media:content url="https://static01.nyt.com/images/2026/11/18/multimedia/18sp-watches-atomic-inyt-02-hfvq/18sp-watches-atomic-inyt-02-hfvq-videoSixteenByNineJumbo1600.jpg" medium="image"><media:credit>Juho Kuva for The New York Times</media:credit><media:description>To measure time, optical clocks use a laser to probe atoms that have been isolated and cooled to a near standstill. The photo shows compact lasers made by the Finnish company Vexlum, which is working with Menlo Systems on commercial production of an optical clock.</media:description></media:content></item></channel></rss>